Ladybird resolves a message’s named placeholders once, as it parses
the catalog, and fills in positional substitutions at each getMessage()
call. Message names are case-insensitive, and a catalog may not
define the six built-in names. Chromium does the same in MessageBundle::
GetMessageValue(), GetPlaceholders(), and ReplaceVariables(), and
its GetI18nMessage() runs base::ReplaceStringPlaceholders() even with no
substitutions. Ladybird follows those two functions on every dollar-sign
edge: single-digit indexes, a run of dollar signs losing one, a “$” and
the character after it dropped, and nothing at all for more than nine
substitutions. Since that drop works on bytes, Ladybird decodes what’s
left as UTF-8 with replacement characters, which is what Chromium’s
gin::StringToV8() makes of the same bytes.

Gecko’s LocaleData.addLocale() replaces an undefined named
placeholder with the empty string, and its localizeMessage()
takes any number of substitutions, with multi-digit indexes, and
keeps a “$” that precedes anything but a digit or another “$”. WebKit’s
WebExtensionLocalization::localizedStringForKey() returns the empty
string for more than nine substitutions. Ladybird deliberately follows
Chromium on all these edges.

For CSS, Ladybird keeps an unknown __MSG_ token as it is and goes
on replacing the rest, as Gecko’s LocaleData.localize() does. Chromium’s
ReplaceVariables() stops at the first unknown name, leaving every later
token unreplaced, and WebKit’s localizedStringForString() replaces an
unknown one with the empty string.
